<header><p class="st-kicker">tribal confederation · British Isles</p><h1 class="st-title">Caledonii</h1><p class="st-meta">80 CE – 300 CE (approx.)</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Confederation of the Scottish Highlands that fought Agricola at Mons Graupius and Septimius Severus, and gave its name to Caledonia.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The Caledonii were a people of the central and northern Highlands, named by Ptolemy and others; Roman writers used <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/new-caledonia-darien">Caledonia</a> for northern <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/united-kingdom">Britain</a> as a whole. They first appear in history when the governor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gnaeus-julius-agricola">Agricola</a> marched north, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tacitus">Tacitus</a> says a host of Caledonian peoples gathered under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/calgacus">Calgacus</a> to meet him at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-mons-graupius">Mons Graupius</a> in 83 or 84.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> describe them as a warrior society of clans and chiefs, without towns or coinage, living by herding and farming in a landscape of roundhouses, crannogs and brochs further north. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cassius-dio">Cassius Dio</a> later portrays the Caledonians and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/maeatae">Maeatae</a> as the two main groupings beyond the walls, and tells of the Caledonian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/argentocoxus">Argentocoxus</a>, whose wife bantered with the empress <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/julia-domna">Julia Domna</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Defeated at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-mons-graupius">Mons Graupius</a> but never conquered, the Caledonians bought peace or raided as Roman power allowed. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/septimius-severus">Septimius Severus</a> campaigned against them from 208 to 211, and after his death <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> withdrew. By the late 3rd century Roman writers spoke instead of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/picts">Picts</a>, whose <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kingdom-of-the-picts">Kingdom of the Picts</a> absorbed the Caledonian peoples.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/caledonii.jpg" alt="Caledonii" width="320" height="464"><figcaption>Image: (myself) · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Britain.north.peoples.Ptolemy.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>80 CE – 300 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/1st-century">1st century</a> – <a href="/centuries/4th-century">4th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>tribal confederation</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Calgacus, Argentocoxus</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Celtic polytheism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Common Brittonic (Pictish)</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/united-kingdom">United Kingdom</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Caledonians, Caledonian Confederacy</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=190&amp;focus=caledonii" aria-label="Caledonii on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4644 307 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Caledonii lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5304 942h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4964 563h0"/><path class="map-dot-ap" d="M4964 563h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>Location approximate: shown in present-day United Kingdom. <a href="/atlas?year=190&amp;focus=caledonii">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
